---
/**
 * Privacy and trust (/privacy/).
 *
 * What: every fact about where Axcess keeps data and what it connects to,
 * then sign-in scans, the optional AI, where the files are, team use,
 * accuracy, and last, this website's own promise. An IT reviewer reads it
 * top to bottom; everyone else jumps from "On this page".
 *
 * Why this order: "what stays and what leaves" is the question that
 * decides whether Axcess may be used at all, so it comes first (U.S.
 * Federal Plain Language Guidelines: lead with the main point). Each later
 * section answers one follow-up question (COGA "Making Content Usable":
 * one idea per chunk, https://www.w3.org/TR/coga-usable/, paraphrased).
 *
 * Every fact was checked against the code (desktop/src/updates.cjs and
 * main.cjs for the update check, src/audit/config.py for Ollama,
 * src/audit/web/server.py and src/audit/protected/ for sign-in scans,
 * docs/desktop-app.md for the folders). Where the old page said more than
 * the code does, the claim is narrowed here, never dropped: for example,
 * Ollama's address can be changed for public scans, so the page says so.
 *
 * Rejected: the old page's two diagrams (privacy-boundary.png,
 * login-scan-flow.png). Their words are out of date ("Site with a login or
 * 2FA", "Don't store rendered pages", "Rule docs"), and a picture whose
 * words differ from the app sends people looking for buttons that do not
 * exist. The lists below carry every fact the diagrams did. Bring them back
 * once docs/images/diagrams/ is redrawn with the app's words.
 */
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import Card from "../components/Card.astro";
import { REPO, url } from "../lib/site";
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "Privacy and trust",
    description:
      "What stays on your computer, what Axcess connects to, and how it scans pages behind a sign-in without ever seeing your password.",
  }}
  headings={[
    { depth: 2, slug: "boundary", text: "What stays on your computer and what connects out" },
    { depth: 3, slug: "stays", text: "Stays on your computer" },
    { depth: 3, slug: "connects", text: "Connects out" },
    { depth: 2, slug: "sign-in", text: "Scanning behind a sign-in without sharing your password" },
    { depth: 2, slug: "ai", text: "AI checks are optional and labelled" },
    { depth: 2, slug: "data", text: "Where your data is kept" },
    { depth: 2, slug: "teams", text: "Using Axcess as a team" },
    { depth: 2, slug: "accuracy", text: "What Axcess claims about its own accuracy" },
    { depth: 2, slug: "this-site", text: "This website" },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Accessibility work often involves private pages and pages behind a sign-in. Those should not be uploaded to
    anyone's cloud. Axcess keeps scans, sign-ins and reports on the computer that runs it. It has no account, no
    tracking (telemetry) and no upload.
  </p>

  <h2 id="boundary">What stays on your computer and what connects out</h2>

  {
    /* Two plain lists, not the old drawn boxes with role="list" on <div>s:
       a native <ul> under a heading gives the same structure without ARIA
       (W3C "Using ARIA", rule 1, https://www.w3.org/TR/using-aria/#rule1,
       paraphrased; SC 1.3.1 Info and Relationships, Level A). Each item
       starts with its name in bold, so the list can be scanned. */
  }
  <h3 id="stays">Stays on your computer</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>What a scan finds.</strong> Pages, elements, their code, screenshots and image files, kept in a local
      database and a folder of files.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your decisions.</strong> Every status change and the reason you gave, with its history.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reports and exports.</strong> Exported files are written to your disk and go only where you send them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The browser.</strong> Chromium opens the pages on your computer, including the window you sign in
      with. The desktop app includes it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Text recognition (OCR).</strong> Reading text inside images happens on your computer. The desktop app
      includes it. If you run Axcess from the source code, you install Tesseract yourself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Optional AI.</strong> If you install Ollama, a program that runs AI models, Axcess uses it on this
      computer by default. <a href="#ai">More about the AI checks</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id="connects">Connects out</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The website you scan.</strong> Axcess loads pages from that site at the speed you set. A public scan
      follows the site's robots.txt file (where a site asks scanners to skip pages) unless you choose to ignore it.
      A sign-in scan does not check robots.txt: your confirmation that the site owner allows the scan takes its
      place. Opening a saved copy of a page later can also load that site's styles, fonts and images.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>GitHub, to check for a newer desktop app.</strong> Each time you open the desktop app, it asks GitHub
      once whether a newer version exists. On a Mac it also asks when you reopen its window. The request carries no
      scan data. Nothing is downloaded unless you choose to update. Setting <code>AXCESS_DISABLE_UPDATE_CHECK=1</code> turns the check off.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Links you choose to open.</strong> <em>Give feedback</em> opens a form in your browser, and <em>About this rule</em> opens the rule's help page. They open only when you select them, and nothing about
      your scan is sent with them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing else.</strong> No tracking, no usage statistics (analytics), no cloud AI built in. Anything
      else would need an administrator to set it up on purpose.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="sign-in">Scanning behind a sign-in without sharing your password</h2>
  <p>
    Many of the pages that matter most are behind a sign-in. Axcess handles this the safe way: you sign in
    yourself, and it scans with that sign-in. U-M sign-in and Duo two-step sign-in work.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Sign-in scans work only when Axcess runs on your own computer. They are not available on a shared server.
    </li>
    <li>The sign-in window is a normal Chromium window with a new, empty browser profile made just for this scan.</li>
    <li>You type your password, passkey or one-time code into the website, never into Axcess.</li>
    <li>
      Axcess keeps the sign-in in memory only. When the scan ends, the sign-in is deleted with the temporary browser
      profile. No password or reusable sign-in is saved.
    </li>
    <li>
      The report on your computer keeps saved copies of the pages you signed in to, and screenshots, unless you
      choose <em>Don’t keep a saved copy of each page</em>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The site's address must start with https:// and point to a public internet address. Sites on a private
      network address can't be scanned this way.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p><a href={url("/get-started/#sign-in")}>Step by step: scan a site behind a sign-in</a>.</p>

  {
    /* The permission reminder is a bordered box with a bold lead-in, not
       colour alone (SC 1.4.1 Use of Color, Level A), because it is a
       safety fact people must not skim past. */
  }
  <div class="not-content ax-prose callout">
    <p>
      <strong>You stay in control of sign-in.</strong> Axcess only continues after you sign in yourself. Use sites
      and accounts you have permission to test.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai">AI checks are optional and labelled</h2>
  {
    /* Shared Card panels in a grid that becomes one column on a narrow
       screen (SC 1.4.10 Reflow, Level AA). Card keeps every panel on the
       site the same (SC 3.2.4 Consistent Identification, Level AA). */
  }
  <div class="not-content ax-prose cards">
    <Card heading="You install it, or you don't">
      <p>
        AI review, and comparing image text with alt text, need Ollama and models you download yourself. Axcess
        never installs them or downloads a model by itself, and every other check works without them.
      </p>
      <p>
        Axcess looks for Ollama on this computer. Whoever sets up Axcess can point a public scan at Ollama on
        another computer (the <code>AUDIT_OLLAMA_BASE_URL</code> setting). A sign-in scan only accepts Ollama on this
        same computer.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card heading="Every AI result is marked">
      <p>
        An AI result is shown as Needs review, with the model's reasons beside the original evidence. It can't
        become a Barrier without a person.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </div>

  <h2 id="data">Where your data is kept</h2>
  <p>
    The desktop app keeps your reports in your system's folder for app data, never inside the app itself. Delete
    the folder and the reports are gone.
  </p>
  {
    /* A real table with a caption and row headers (SC 1.3.1 Info and
       Relationships, Level A). The folder paths may break anywhere, so the
       table fits at 320 px and needs no sideways scrolling (SC 1.4.10
       Reflow, Level AA). */
  }
  <table class="folders">
    <caption>Where the desktop app keeps your reports</caption>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">System</th><th scope="col">Folder</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">macOS</th><td><code>~/Library/Application Support/Axcess/data/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Windows</th><td><code>%APPDATA%/Axcess/data/</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Windows, portable zip</th><td><code>Axcess data/data/</code>, next to <code>Axcess.exe</code></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Linux</th><td><code>~/.config/Axcess/data/</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Inside the folder:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>one database (SQLite), which is the record of every report;</li>
    <li>a <code>blobs</code> folder of images and screenshots;</li>
    <li>logs, which include the address and title of every page scanned.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The files are not encrypted, so treat the folder with the same care as the pages you scanned. Deleting a report
    keeps its image and screenshot files. Exported files are copies, not the record.
  </p>

  <h2 id="teams">Using Axcess as a team</h2>
  <div class="not-content ax-prose cards">
    <Card heading="A small team on a private network">
      <p>
        Axcess can run on an always-on computer for a trusted team, on a private network or a private link such as
        Tailscale, behind a shared access code. The code only lets people in. There are no separate accounts.
        Sign-in scans are not available there.
      </p>
      <p>
        Never make it an open public address: anyone who reaches it can point the scanner at any site. <a href={url("/docs/hosting/")}>Hosting Axcess</a> explains the safe setups.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card heading="Protected scans run by IT">
      <p>
        For sensitive university systems there is a stricter setup that IT runs. People reach it only through an
        approved university sign-in. Its evidence is encrypted and deleted after seven days, and exports leave out
        sensitive details. It needs university systems to run, and it is off unless IT turns it on.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </div>

  <h2 id="accuracy">What Axcess claims about its own accuracy</h2>
  <div class="not-content ax-prose cards">
    <Card heading="A safety check, not a sales number">
      <p>
        The project keeps a fixed set of made-up examples, each labelled with the right answer, and scores the
        checks against it. For each kind of check, fewer than 5% of the problems it reports may be wrong, and it
        must find at least 80% of the real problems in the set.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the examples are made up, this protects the rules for what counts as a Barrier. It does not measure
        accuracy on real sites.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card heading="What that does not mean">
      <p>
        It does not promise the same results on every real website. A real-world accuracy figure would need a new,
        typical sample of real pages, checked separately by at least two accessibility experts. The project says so
        in writing.
      </p>
    </Card>
  </div>

  <h2 id="this-site">This website</h2>
  <p>
    Every page can be read and navigated without JavaScript. Search and the light or dark switch need it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The site loads no fonts, trackers or scripts from other companies. Its fonts are stored on the site itself. The
    site is hosted on GitHub Pages, so GitHub delivers each page to your browser.
  </p>
  <p>
    The only other request is optional. On <a href={url("/get-started/#download")}>the download section</a>, your
    browser asks GitHub for the newest release, so each button can point straight at its file. If scripts are off,
    no request is made and the buttons open the release page on GitHub instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    Links to GitHub, such as "Edit page" and the source code at <a href={REPO}>github.com/lsa-mis/axcess</a>, open
    only when you select them.
  </p>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
  .lede {
    color: var(--ax-fg-muted);
    font-size: 1.1875rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .callout {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    border: 2px solid var(--ax-border-strong);
    border-inline-start: 6px solid var(--ax-maize);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    background: var(--ax-surface-subtle);
  }
  .callout p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--ax-fg);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
  .cards {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 16rem), 1fr));
    gap: 1rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }
  .cards code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
    border-radius: 0.25rem;
    background: var(--ax-surface-muted);
    color: var(--ax-fg);
    font-family: var(--sl-font-mono);
    font-size: 0.875em;
  }
  /* The caption is visible: unlike the home page's table there is no
     heading right above that says what the table holds. */
  .folders {
    display: table;
    width: 100%;
    table-layout: fixed;
  }
  .folders caption {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--ax-fg);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-align: start;
  }
  .folders th[scope="col"]:first-child {
    width: 38%;
  }
  .folders th,
  .folders td {
    vertical-align: top;
    text-align: start;
  }
  .folders code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    word-break: break-word;
  }
</style>
